# Instructions for coding agents

This is a Databricks App: a React single-page app served by AppKit's Express
server, written in TypeScript. Every contributor starts with only this
repository, so everything that matters lives in it.

**"AppKit" here always means Databricks' TypeScript SDK:** `@databricks/appkit`
for the server and its plugins, and `@databricks/appkit-ui` for the React
components and the hooks that load data. It is not Apple's AppKit framework,
and Apple's documentation answers the wrong questions. Look things up with
`npx @databricks/appkit docs <query>`, or at
https://databricks.github.io/appkit/llms.txt.

## How work is done here

- **The tools are the guardrail.** A rule that a tool can check is a check, and
  the build fails on it. Zero errors and zero warnings, always.
- **An exception to a rule is debt, and its reason is written down** where a
  reviewer will see it.
- **Test first.** Work outside in: a scenario, then a failing test at the
  cheapest level that proves the rule, then the code.
- **Model the domain.** Business rules live in plain, immutable domain objects
  named in the business's own words.
- **Small commits,** each one passing the whole gate.

## Commands

| Command                        | What it does                                                                |
| ------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `mise install`, `pnpm install` | Install the pinned tools, then the dependencies                             |
| `mise run hooks`               | Install the git hooks (`check` before each commit) and restore the skills   |
| `pnpm run check`               | The whole gate: every check below except the smoke journeys                 |
| `pnpm run lint`                | Linters, import rules, structural rules, `appkit lint`, SQL lint, sizes     |
| `pnpm run typecheck`           | `tsc -b`                                                                    |
| `pnpm run format:fix`          | Formats everything with Prettier                                            |
| `pnpm test`                    | Unit, property and page-level tests, with coverage and accessibility checks |
| `pnpm run test:mutation`       | Mutation testing of the code whose tests run in Node                        |
| `pnpm run test:e2e`            | Journeys through the built app, with Databricks faked; build first          |
| `pnpm run test:smoke`          | Smoke journeys against a deployed app; needs `APP_URL`                      |
| `pnpm run typegen`             | Regenerates the types for the SQL files in `config/queries/`                |

`mise run hooks` fails while git's `core.hooksPath` is set, as a global git
configuration can set it. Unset it, or set it to `.git/hooks`, first.

The Databricks CLI runs `typegen`, `lint:ast-grep`, `typecheck`, `build` and
`test` by name when it validates the project, so keep those names.

## The shape of the code

| Layer       | Folders                                       | Holds                                                                                        |
| ----------- | --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| View        | `client/src/pages/`, `client/src/components/` | Function components. No business logic, and no `fetch`                                       |
| Application | `client/src/acl/`                             | The only code that calls `fetch`, names `/api/` paths, or uses AppKit's data hooks and types |
| Domain      | `client/src/domain/`                          | Plain TypeScript classes and functions. Nothing from React, AppKit or the browser            |
| Server      | `server/`                                     | AppKit's server, its plugins, and the app's own routes                                       |

The folders `acl/`, `domain/` and `server/` have their own `AGENTS.md`; read it
before editing there. Why the layers exist, and the exceptions AppKit's own
files need, are in `docs/design/architecture.md`.

One exception in the view: AppKit's chart and table components, and its
`GenieChat`, fetch for themselves, and a view may render them as they are.

**No request waterfalls.** Details and examples are in
`docs/design/data-loading.md`.

- Data from the app's own endpoints is requested in route loaders, which call
  functions in `acl/`. Every loader on a page starts at once, before it renders.
  A child route's loader never waits for its parent's data.
- AppKit's data hooks can't run in a loader. A page calls their `acl/` hooks in
  its own body, or in children it always renders; a component that loads data
  never sits behind another query's spinner.
- Prefer one SQL query with a join to several queries that depend on one
  another. Each trip to a SQL warehouse costs seconds.

## Pages and accessibility

- **Each page owns its one `h1`;** the layout supplies none, and the header's
  app name is plain text. A page without one fails axe's `page-has-heading-one`.
- **When a component renders its title as a `div`** (AppKit's `CardTitle` and
  `EmptyTitle`), give it the level with `role="heading"` and `aria-level`, so
  its look stays.
- **Scenarios find a page's title** with `getByRole('heading', { level: 1 })`.
- Axe runs the WCAG rules and `page-has-heading-one`. Its other best-practice
  rules are off; turn one on in `accessibilityRules` in `features/tasks/visitor.ts`.

## Never

- `fetch` inside `useEffect`, or anywhere in the client outside `acl/`.
- `useMemo` or `useCallback`. The React Compiler memoizes.
- Changing a domain object. Write `order.withStatus(s)`, which returns a new
  object, not `order.status = s`.
- A custom endpoint for a warehouse `SELECT` or for Unity Catalog file
  operations. Put the query in `config/queries/` and read it with AppKit's
  hooks. Custom endpoints are for writes, Databricks APIs that have no plugin,
  logic over several steps, external services and file processing.
- Lakebase SQL in `config/queries/`. That folder is for the SQL warehouse only.
- Hand-written types for SQL results. Run `pnpm run typegen`.
- Next.js, React Router's framework mode, or any other server-rendering
  framework. AppKit's server is the server (`docs/decisions/`, record 0001).
- An `async` Express handler without `asyncHandler` from
  `server/async-handler.ts`. AppKit runs Express 4, which loses its errors.
- A route without `errorElement: <RouteError />`.
- A plain `<form>`. Use React Router's `<Form>` for a form that only submits to
  an action, and React Hook Form with Zod for one that validates in the browser.
- `--force`, an override or a downgrade to make an install succeed. When an
  install fails, leave it failed and report the error.
- A write path (Lakebase, `INSERT`s into Delta, or another system) before the
  team chooses where writes go (`docs/open-questions.md`).

## Escape hatches

A disable comment, `@ts-expect-error`, a skipped or focused test, a coverage or
mutation exclusion, or an entry in a tool's ignore list hides a problem instead
of fixing it. Change the code so the tool is satisfied. If that truly can't be
done, put the reason on the same line as the exception, and repeat it in the
commit message.

## Where knowledge lives

| Knowledge                                          | Home                                                                       |
| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Rules for every agent                              | This file                                                                  |
| Rules for one layer                                | `AGENTS.md` in that layer's folder                                         |
| Decisions that would take a rewrite to undo        | `docs/decisions/`, written with the `adr` skill                            |
| How each part works, and the reasons for each tool | `docs/design/`                                                             |
| Platform facts, each with the date it was checked  | `docs/design/platform.md`                                                  |
| Questions only the team can answer                 | `docs/open-questions.md`                                                   |
| The work ahead                                     | `docs/plan.md`                                                             |
| Procedures worth repeating                         | `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, written with the `skill-authoring` skill |

Shared skills (Databricks' own, `adr`, `skill-authoring`) are pinned in
`skills-lock.json` and installed by a git hook; never commit their files.

## Definition of Done

- The gate is green: `pnpm run check` reports no error and no warning.
- Every new test fails before the change, checks one behavior precisely, and
  leaves no mutant alive.
- Any document the change makes untrue is fixed in the same commit.
- An item in `docs/plan.md` that the work finishes is ticked.
- The proposed commit message is a Conventional Commit, and says why the change
  was made, not which files it touched.

## Before you stop

Put everything the next contributor will need in its home in the table above,
and commit it. A conversation, a scratch file and an agent's own memory are
gone by the next session.

## Committing

What agents may do with git without asking is the team's decision, and it is
still open (`docs/open-questions.md`). Until it is answered, commit and push
nothing without asking.
